THE FINGERPRINT

What follows adds no condition to the MIT grant above. It takes nothing away
from it either. It describes how this code is identified, so that nobody has to
be told twice.

Run every function in this device over every face and write the answers down in
order. You get twenty two characters:

    01u10u000u0u11110u1uu1

That is the fingerprint. It is not stored in a comment, a header, a string, or
a file. It is what the code does, read back out, so it survives deleting the
comments, renaming everything, reformatting, minifying, and porting to another
language. `node burn.mjs` in this repository does all of that and prints it.

Hash it and you get the key:

    sha256("01u10u000u0u11110u1uu1") = 66cce8d50854...

The key resolves to the list of everyone who worked on this, which is published
in README.md and at https://chuumind.com. Those records are not inside your
copy, so removing the notice from your copy does not remove them.

You may burn it off. The MIT grant does ask you to keep the notice, and if you
drop it that is between you and the licence, but nothing technical stops you and
nothing here pretends otherwise. What it buys you is the time between your
deletion and somebody running the device and hashing the answer. Spend it well.

Change the tables and the key changes with them. At that point it is a different
device and it is yours. That was never restricted.

The method is published on purpose. Nothing here depends on anybody not knowing
how it works.
